ntfs-macos patch 0006: session channel on NTFSMAC_ERRFD (S, E, W, U records)

Rationale (T-M3-18, ADR-018, ARCHITECTURE.md section 7): the daemon must learn of a device write
error independently of FSKit, and must see positive proof that a session ended cleanly. Stock
ntfs-3g exits 0 after every return from fuse_loop() and only logs a failed ntfs_umount(), so neither
its exit status nor silence is evidence. This patch writes fixed-format ASCII records, one write()
each, at most 32 bytes with the newline, to the descriptor named by the environment variable
NTFSMAC_ERRFD:
  S rw|ro          src/ntfs-3g.c main(), right after mount_fuse() succeeded and ctx->mounted = TRUE;
                   "ro" when NVolReadOnly(ctx->vol) (the engine's own read-only fallback included).
                   At most once.
  E <op> <errno>   libntfs-3g/unix_io.c: a device write that failed or was short (op pwrite, write),
                   a failed device sync (sync), a failed sync or close() in the device close (close);
                   src/ntfs-3g.c ntfs_close(): ntfs_umount() returned non-zero (umount).
  W flush <errno>  libntfs-3g/unix_io.c ntfs_fsync(): the drive flush failed and only fsync() was done.
                   In this patch: the fcntl(F_FULLFSYNC) fallback (an image file on macOS, errno of the
                   failed fcntl). Patch 0007 adds the same record for a failed DKIOCSYNCHRONIZE on a
                   device node. A report only: the return value of ntfs_fsync() does not change.
  U <n>            src/ntfs-3g.c ntfs_close(), only when ntfs_umount() returned 0 and S was written; n is
                   the number of E records this process tried to write (written or not).
Record text is a literal in unix_io.c plus decimal integers; nothing comes from the disk.
An E record means "a device call failed or returned a short count". ntfs_pwrite() in libntfs-3g/device.c
loops until the whole buffer is written, so a single short write that the engine then completes still
writes an E record and makes the session unclean (M3 audit F-08). Nothing is lost in that case; the next
attach is read-only and the user is told to run chkdsk. This fails closed and is kept on purpose: the
channel cannot tell a short write that is completed from one that is not.
Hunks:
  - libntfs-3g/unix_io.c: the channel (init, write, four exported functions ntfsmac_errfd_session,
    _error, _warn, _unmounted), declared in include/ntfs-3g/device.h. The E records come from four
    wrapper operations (ntfsmac_unix_io_close, _write, _pwrite, _sync) that call the upstream
    functions and are put in ntfs_device_unix_io_ops; the upstream functions and the lines patch 0004
    changes (the write, pwrite and ntfs_fsync call sites) are not edited, so this patch applies with
    and without 0004 and the Linux object file of a release build equals the series-without-0004
    reference (tests/stress/crashsweep/build.sh). Read-only devices write no E record. W is written
    from ntfs_fsync(), inside the __APPLE__ branch.
  - src/ntfs-3g.c: S after mount_fuse(), U or "E umount" in ntfs_close() (a few lines).
Channel rules (ARCHITECTURE.md section 7, "Bounds and descriptor hygiene"):
  - The variable is read when the device layer first needs it (ntfs_device_unix_io_open(), before
    mount_fuse()). Only a decimal descriptor 3..100000 that is a pipe or a socket and is writable is
    accepted; a device, a regular file or anything else is ignored, so a record can never reach a
    disk. Ignored or unset: no output, no change in behaviour.
  - FD_CLOEXEC is set and the descriptor is made O_NONBLOCK; the variable is removed from the environment
    once the descriptor is accepted, so children do not see it. A full pipe drops the record and the
    process never blocks. A reader that went away gives EPIPE and must not kill the worker: on macOS
    F_SETNOSIGPIPE is set, and when that works SIGPIPE is not raised at all; otherwise SIGPIPE is blocked
    around the write and a SIGPIPE that the write raised is consumed (sigtimedwait with a zero timeout on
    Linux, so it cannot block). EINTR is retried a few times, nothing else is retried.
  - At most 30 E and 30 W records are written (with S and U below the daemon's 64); more are counted
    for U only. errno is preserved by every function.
  - Faultio builds only (ENABLE_FAULTIO, patch 0004): if NTFSMAC_FAULT_ERRFD_CMD is set, the channel
    flags are printed to stderr and that command is run through system(), for I-ERR-07. The code and
    strings are not compiled into a release build.
Scope: device layer and two places in the FUSE front end; nothing touches allocation, runlists, MFT or
index code. Used by every program linked with libntfs-3g (ntfsfix, mkntfs, the probe, volmark), but they
write a record only when the variable names a pipe or socket. Series order: after 0003 and 0004, before
0007, 0008 and 0009 (re-based onto it by T-M3-18; 0007 gains the W call for DKIOCSYNCHRONIZE).
Upstream status: not submitted (specific to the ntfs-macos daemon protocol).
Test: I-ERR-01..07 (tests/integration/errfd/i_err.py); I-DMN-24 and T-M4-13 for the daemon side.

